---
import CaseSection from "../components/case-study/CaseSection.astro";

const degreeFlows = [
  {
    number: "03.1",
    degree: "S1",
    title: "Undergraduate monitoring",
    src: "/images/projects/monitoring/flowchart-s1.png",
    alt: "S1 undergraduate student monitoring flowchart",
    milestones: ["KKN", "Proposal examination", "Thesis examination"],
    description:
      "The undergraduate flow evaluates mandatory milestones in sequence and uses separate reminder and overdue-warning windows before adding a student to the monitoring list.",
  },
  {
    number: "03.2",
    degree: "S2",
    title: "Master’s monitoring",
    src: "/images/projects/monitoring/flowchart-s2.png",
    alt: "S2 master's student monitoring flowchart",
    milestones: ["Proposal examination", "Thesis seminar", "Thesis examination"],
    description:
      "The master’s flow follows a different progression and timeline, so its academic checkpoints are evaluated independently from the undergraduate rules.",
  },
  {
    number: "03.3",
    degree: "S3",
    title: "Doctoral monitoring",
    src: "/images/projects/monitoring/flowchart-s3.png",
    alt: "S3 doctoral student monitoring flowchart",
    milestones: ["Comprehensive examination", "Assessment", "UTUP"],
    description:
      "The doctoral flow uses its own mandatory agenda sequence and reminder logic, demonstrating why one generic rule could not represent all degree levels.",
  },
];

const roleViews = [
  {
    title: "Dean / Faculty",
    scope: "Faculty-wide access",
    src: "/images/projects/monitoring/role-dean-redacted.png",
    alt: "Dean role view of the production student monitoring system",
    description:
      "Faculty leadership can review troubled-student records across the faculty rather than being restricted to one program or department.",
  },
  {
    title: "Department Head",
    scope: "Department-level access",
    src: "/images/projects/monitoring/role-department-head-redacted.png",
    alt: "Department head role view of the production student monitoring system",
    description:
      "Department leadership receives the same monitoring structure with the dataset restricted to programs inside its department.",
  },
  {
    title: "Program Head",
    scope: "Study-program access",
    src: "/images/projects/monitoring/role-program-head-redacted.png",
    alt: "Program head role view of the production student monitoring system",
    description:
      "Program heads and secretaries only receive monitoring data for students within their own study program.",
  },
  {
    title: "Supervisor / Promoter",
    scope: "Supervised students only",
    src: "/images/projects/monitoring/role-supervisor-redacted.png",
    alt: "Academic supervisor role view of the production student monitoring system",
    description:
      "Academic supervisors and promoter teams see only students assigned to them, keeping the same feature useful without exposing unrelated records.",
  },
];

const testResults = [
  ["Login system", "Valid"],
  ["Dean filtering", "Valid"],
  ["Department filtering", "Valid"],
  ["Program filtering", "Valid"],
  ["Supervisor filtering", "Valid"],
  ["Dashboard Stats", "Valid"],
  ["Pagination", "Valid"],
  ["Email notification", "Valid"],
  ["Excel export", "Troubleshoot"],
];
---

<!-- =======================================================
     01 / CONTEXT
     ======================================================= -->

<CaseSection
  number="01"
  label="Context"
  title="Detecting academic delays before they become easy to overlook."
  intro="FMIPA UGM needed an integrated way to identify students who were late completing mandatory academic agendas, notify the people responsible for them, and give academic leadership a clearer monitoring overview."
>
  <p>
    Before the feature existed, identifying potentially troubled students relied heavily on
    manual review. Without an automated monitoring mechanism, early detection could become
    slow, inconsistent, or simply be overlooked.
  </p>

  <p>
    The module was developed inside the existing Persuratan FMIPA system rather than as a
    separate application. Its core responsibilities were to detect delayed mandatory academic
    agendas, send email warnings, expose detailed monitoring information, and generate data
    that academic staff could use for follow-up.
  </p>

  <p>
    The resulting project therefore combined UI/UX design, business-rule mapping, relational
    data processing, role-based access, backend implementation, reporting, notification logic,
    and functional testing within an existing institutional system.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<!-- =======================================================
     SYSTEM SCOPE
     ======================================================= -->

<section
  class="site-container py-12 md:py-16"
  data-reveal
>
  <div class="editorial-grid">

    <!-- Label -->

    <div>
      <p class="mono-label text-muted">
        System Scope
      </p>
    </div>


    <!-- Scope cards -->

    <div
      class="editorial-content grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-4"
    >

      <!-- Degree flows -->

      <article
        class="border border-line p-6 md:p-7"
      >
        <p
          class="font-mono text-[9px] uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-muted"
        >
          S1 · S2 · S3
        </p>

        <h3
          class="mt-4 text-[clamp(1.5rem,2vw,2rem)] leading-[1.05] font-medium tracking-[-0.035em]"
        >
          Degree flows
        </h3>

        <p
          class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-muted"
        >
          Separate monitoring logic for undergraduate,
          master's, and doctoral programs.
        </p>
      </article>


      <!-- Access scopes -->

      <article
        class="border border-line p-6 md:p-7"
      >
        <p
          class="font-mono text-[9px] uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-muted"
        >
          4 access levels
        </p>

        <h3
          class="mt-4 text-[clamp(1.5rem,2vw,2rem)] leading-[1.05] font-medium tracking-[-0.035em]"
        >
          Access scopes
        </h3>

        <p
          class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-muted"
        >
          Faculty, department, program, and
          supervisor-level data filtering.
        </p>
      </article>


      <!-- Core features -->

      <article
        class="border border-line p-6 md:p-7"
      >
        <p
          class="font-mono text-[9px] uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-muted"
        >
          5 core functions
        </p>

        <h3
          class="mt-4 text-[clamp(1.5rem,2vw,2rem)] leading-[1.05] font-medium tracking-[-0.035em]"
        >
          Core features
        </h3>

        <p
          class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-muted"
        >
          Statistics, monitoring, notifications,
          reporting, and system integration.
        </p>
      </article>


      <!-- Warning stages -->

      <article
        class="border border-line p-6 md:p-7"
      >
        <p
          class="font-mono text-[9px] uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-muted"
        >
          2 warning stages
        </p>

        <h3
          class="mt-4 text-[clamp(1.5rem,2vw,2rem)] leading-[1.05] font-medium tracking-[-0.035em]"
        >
          Warning flow
        </h3>

        <p
          class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-muted"
        >
          Reminder first, followed by an overdue
          warning after the required deadline.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- =======================================================
     02 / REQUIREMENTS + PROTOTYPE
     ======================================================= -->

<CaseSection
  number="02"
  label="Requirements"
  title="Academic policy had to be translated into rules the system could actually evaluate."
>
  <p>
    The project began with understanding the existing Persuratan environment and its technical
    structure. I was introduced to PHP CodeIgniter 4, MySQL, and the MVC architecture used by
    the system, then explored the existing codebase with the programmer responsible for
    maintaining Persuratan FMIPA.
  </p>

  <p>
    Requirement discovery then moved beyond the IT team. I communicated with the project
    initiator and academic administration staff to identify the mandatory agendas used to
    evaluate student progress at different degree levels.
  </p>

  <p>
    The findings were turned into an early UI/UX prototype and process flows. The prototype was
    used as a communication tool with the project initiator, MBKM mentor, and academic staff
    before development continued iteratively.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<section class="site-container py-10 md:py-16" data-reveal>
  <div class="editorial-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="mono-label text-muted">Early Prototype</p>
    </div>

    <div class="editorial-content">
      <figure>
        <div class="overflow-hidden border border-line bg-[#ecebe6] p-4 md:p-8">
          <img
            src="/images/projects/monitoring/early-prototype.png"
            alt="Early UI prototype for the FMIPA student monitoring system"
            loading="lazy"
            class="mx-auto block h-auto w-full max-w-[1100px] border border-black/10 bg-white"
          />
        </div>

        <figcaption class="mt-4 max-w-[800px] text-[12px] leading-[1.75] text-muted">
          Early dashboard prototype used to validate the feature direction before the final
          production implementation was integrated into Persuratan FMIPA.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


<!-- =======================================================
     03 / DETECTION LOGIC
     ======================================================= -->

<CaseSection
  number="03"
  label="Detection Logic"
  title="One generic rule was not enough for three different academic pathways."
>
  <p>
    The system collects student academic records, supervisor or promoter relationships, and
    completed mandatory agendas from the existing data environment. Those records are compared
    against parameters agreed with academic staff to determine whether a student should appear
    in the troubled-student list.
  </p>

  <p>
    S1, S2, and S3 students follow different milestone sequences and timelines. I therefore
    mapped an independent flow for each degree level rather than forcing all students through a
    single detection rule.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each flow checks whether the required agenda has already been completed, evaluates the
    student's academic timing, and decides whether the system should send a reminder, issue an
    overdue warning, or continue checking the next agenda.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

{degreeFlows.map((flow, index) => (
  <section class="site-container py-10 md:py-14" data-reveal>
    <div class="editorial-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="mono-label text-muted">{flow.number} / {flow.degree}</p>
      </div>

      <div class="editorial-content">
        <div
          class:list={[
            "grid items-start gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,0.42fr)_minmax(0,0.58fr)] lg:gap-12",
            index % 2 === 1 && "lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,0.58fr)_minmax(0,0.42fr)]",
          ]}
        >
          <div class:list={[index % 2 === 1 && "lg:order-2"]}>
            <div class="bg-[#151615] p-4 md:p-7">
              <img
                src={flow.src}
                alt={flow.alt}
                loading="lazy"
                class="mx-auto block h-auto w-full max-w-[600px]"
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class:list={["pt-1", index % 2 === 1 && "lg:order-1"]}>
            <p class="mono-label text-muted">{flow.degree}</p>
            <h3 class="mt-5 max-w-[620px] text-[clamp(2rem,3vw,3.25rem)] leading-[1.05] font-medium tracking-[-0.04em]">
              {flow.title}
            </h3>

            <p class="mt-6 max-w-[620px] text-[14px] leading-[1.8] text-muted">
              {flow.description}
            </p>

            <div class="mt-8 border-t border-line">
              {flow.milestones.map((milestone, milestoneIndex) => (
                <div class="flex items-center gap-5 border-b border-line py-4">
                  <span class="font-mono text-[9px] text-muted">0{milestoneIndex + 1}</span>
                  <span class="text-[13px]">{milestone}</span>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
))}


<!-- =======================================================
     04 / ARCHITECTURE + DATA
     ======================================================= -->

<CaseSection
  number="04"
  label="Architecture & Data"
  title="The visible dashboard depended on reconstructing useful information from an existing relational data model."
>
  <p>
    The module was implemented with PHP CodeIgniter 4 using MVC, MySQL for data storage and
    processing, and HTML, CSS, and JavaScript for the frontend. The feature was designed to
    integrate directly into Persuratan FMIPA and communicate with the system's existing data
    environment.
  </p>

  <p>
    Much of the information required by the monitoring feature was not stored as one clean,
    ready-to-display dataset. Academic records, supervisor relationships, employee data, user
    accounts, agenda completion, and program statistics had to be connected and processed
    before the interface could show a reliable result.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<section class="site-container py-10 md:py-16" data-reveal>
  <div class="editorial-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="mono-label text-muted">System Architecture</p>
    </div>

    <div class="editorial-content">
      <div class="bg-[#191b19] p-5 text-white md:p-8 lg:p-10">
