        <code class="font-mono text-[10px]">probstud_prodi</code>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[12px] leading-[1.65] text-muted">Troubled-student totals used by Dashboard Stats for program-level visualization.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


<!-- =======================================================
     05 / PRODUCTION INTERFACE
     ======================================================= -->

<CaseSection
  number="05"
  label="Production Interface"
  title="The final module turns monitoring logic into information academic staff can scan and act on."
>
  <p>
    The interface was designed around two main views. Dashboard Stats provides a quick bar-chart
    overview of troubled-student totals by study program, while the detailed monitoring table
    exposes the information required for follow-up: academic timing, delayed agenda,
    supervisor, program, and department.
  </p>

  <p>
    The UI was intentionally kept simple and direct because it lives inside an existing
    administrative system. Navigation and responsive behavior were designed to keep the module
    usable on desktop and tablet without visually separating it from the surrounding Persuratan
    environment.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<section class="site-container py-10 md:py-16" data-reveal>
  <div class="editorial-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="mono-label text-muted">Dashboard Stats</p>
    </div>

    <div class="editorial-content">
      <figure>
        <div class="overflow-hidden border border-line bg-[#ecebe6] p-4 md:p-7 lg:p-9">
          <img
            src="/images/projects/monitoring/dashboard-stats.png"
            alt="Production Dashboard Stats showing troubled students by study program"
            loading="lazy"
            class="block h-auto w-full border border-black/10 bg-white"
          />
        </div>

        <figcaption class="mt-4 max-w-[800px] text-[12px] leading-[1.75] text-muted">
          Production Dashboard Stats. The implementation report documents this view as a bar
          chart of troubled-student totals per study program; at the documented stage, the
          statistics view was still limited to S1 and did not yet include a degree-level selector.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<CaseSection
  number="06"
  label="Role-Based Access"
  title="The interface stays consistent while the visible dataset changes with organizational responsibility."
>
  <p>
    Login credentials are translated into server-side filtering rules. Dean, Vice Dean, and
    administrator-level access can review the broader faculty dataset, while narrower roles are
    restricted to the academic scope they are responsible for.
  </p>

  <p>
    Department leadership is limited to its department, program leadership is limited to its
    study program, and academic supervisors or promoter teams only receive records belonging to
    students under their supervision.
  </p>

  <p>
    This allowed one monitoring module to support several institutional levels while avoiding
    unnecessary exposure of unrelated student records.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<section class="site-container py-10 md:py-16" data-reveal>
  <div class="editorial-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="mono-label text-muted">Production Views</p>
    </div>

    <div class="editorial-content grid gap-x-5 gap-y-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
      {roleViews.map((role) => (
        <figure>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-line bg-white">
            <img
              src={role.src}
              alt={role.alt}
              loading="lazy"
              class="block h-auto w-full"
            />
          </div>

          <figcaption class="mt-4">
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
              <p class="text-[14px] font-medium">{role.title}</p>
              <p class="font-mono text-[9px] uppercase tracking-[0.06em] text-muted">{role.scope}</p>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 max-w-[620px] text-[12px] leading-[1.7] text-muted">{role.description}</p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="site-container py-4 md:py-8" data-reveal>
  <div class="editorial-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="mono-label text-muted">Privacy Note</p>
    </div>

    <div class="editorial-content border-y border-line py-6">
      <p class="max-w-[900px] text-[13px] leading-[1.8] text-muted">
        These are production screenshots from an internal academic system. Only personally
        sensitive table values are obscured for public presentation; role identity, interface
        structure, table headers, and non-sensitive monitoring fields are intentionally left
        visible so the implementation can still be evaluated.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


<!-- =======================================================
     07 / OPERATIONAL FEATURES
     ======================================================= -->

<CaseSection
  number="07"
  label="Operational Features"
  title="Detection was only useful if staff could navigate the result, communicate it, and take the data forward."
>
  <h3>Pagination and sorting</h3>
  <p>
    Pagination was added so the interface did not load an unnecessarily large number of
    records at once, improving efficiency while keeping the monitoring table visually cleaner.
    Compatible table fields also support sorting to make analysis faster.
  </p>

  <h3>Two-stage email notification</h3>
  <p>
    The notification feature sends both the student and their academic supervisor or promoter
    team a first reminder before the required agenda becomes overdue. If the agenda is later
    confirmed as overdue, a stronger warning is sent.
  </p>

  <p>
    Email delivery is synchronized with database updates and warning logs so the application
    can keep track of previous notifications rather than treating every message as a first
    warning.
  </p>

  <h3>Excel reporting</h3>
  <p>
    A detailed-report export was implemented with PHPSpreadsheet for administrative use. The
    feature worked, but testing exposed a pagination-related limitation: the downloaded file
    contained only the records from the currently active page instead of the complete dataset.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<section class="site-container py-10 md:py-14" data-reveal>
  <div class="editorial-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="mono-label text-muted">Pagination UI</p>
    </div>

    <div class="editorial-content">
      <div class="max-w-[760px] border border-line bg-white p-6 md:p-10">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/monitoring/pagination-ui.png"
          alt="Production pagination controls for the student monitoring table"
          loading="lazy"
          class="block h-auto w-full"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="site-container py-10 md:py-14" data-reveal>
  <div class="editorial-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="mono-label text-muted">Notification Flow</p>
    </div>

    <div class="editorial-content">
      <div class="grid gap-3 md:grid-cols-[1fr_auto_1fr_auto_1fr] md:items-stretch">
        <article class="border border-line p-6">
          <p class="mono-label text-muted">01 / Detect</p>
          <h3 class="mt-5 text-xl font-medium tracking-[-0.03em]">Agenda approaching</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-[12px] leading-[1.7] text-muted">Monitoring logic determines that the required agenda has not yet been completed.</p>
        </article>

        <div class="hidden items-center text-muted md:flex">→</div>

        <article class="border border-line p-6">
          <p class="mono-label text-muted">02 / Remind</p>
          <h3 class="mt-5 text-xl font-medium tracking-[-0.03em]">First email</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-[12px] leading-[1.7] text-muted">Student and supervisor or promoter receive a reminder before the agenda becomes overdue.</p>
        </article>

        <div class="hidden items-center text-muted md:flex">→</div>

        <article class="bg-[#191b19] p-6 text-white">
          <p class="mono-label text-white/45">03 / Escalate</p>
          <h3 class="mt-5 text-xl font-medium tracking-[-0.03em]">Overdue warning</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-[12px] leading-[1.7] text-white/55">If the deadline is missed, the second warning is sent and the notification state is updated.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


<!-- =======================================================
     08 / TESTING + LIMITATIONS
     ======================================================= -->

<CaseSection
  number="08"
  label="Testing"
  title="The final validation checked the feature as an integrated workflow, not only as an interface."
>
  <p>
    Functional testing used a black-box approach focused on whether each module produced the
    expected result from representative inputs. Browser validation was performed with Chrome
    and Firefox, and MySQL Workbench was used to verify database integrity and query results.
    PHPUnit was identified as an optional tool for validating selected backend helpers or
    filtering logic.
  </p>

  <p>
    The tested modules covered login, role-based filtering, Dashboard Stats, pagination, Excel
    export, and automatic email notifications. The final integration results recorded all core
    modules as successful and valid except the documented Excel-export pagination issue.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<section class="site-container py-10 md:py-14" data-reveal>
  <div class="editorial-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="mono-label text-muted">Result Summary</p>
    </div>

    <div class="editorial-content">
      <div class="border-t border-line">
        {testResults.map(([feature, status], index) => (
          <div class="grid grid-cols-[48px_1fr_auto] items-center gap-4 border-b border-line py-4">
            <span class="font-mono text-[9px] text-muted">{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
            <span class="text-[13px]">{feature}</span>
            <span
              class:list={[
